'''Uruwa Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|浦和県}}, ''Uruwa-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region on the southern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of 567,877 as of 2026, it is a compact coastal prefecture known for its {{nihongo|tuna|鮪|maguro}} fishing fleet—the most productive in Okaiken—and for its pearl aquaculture operations in the sheltered bays of the southern coast. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Uruwa]], a deepwater port and the centre of Okaiken's tuna auction industry.

Uruwa Prefecture is also the home of the {{nihongo|Uruwa Tuna Exchange|浦和鮪取引所|Uruwa Maguro Torihikijo}}, the nation's primary wholesale market for bluefin tuna, where the annual New Year auction regularly sets record prices that are reported in financial media across Adonia.

== History ==

=== The tuna hunters ===
Uruwa's relationship with the bluefin tuna is ancient and defining. Archaeological evidence from the {{nihongo|Shirasagi|白鷺}} shell midden indicates that tuna fishing was practised in Uruwa's waters as early as 2000 BC, and the {{nihongo|Uruwa Tuna Guild|浦和鮪組合|Uruwa Maguro Kumiai}}, established in 1452, is one of the oldest commercial organisations in Yagikoshu.

The development of the {{nihongo|''nori-bune''|乗り舟}}—a specialised tuna-fishing vessel with a reinforced hull and a raised bow for navigating the heavy seas of the southern Shendan Ocean—in the 16th century transformed Uruwa from a regional fishing port into a major commercial centre. The {{nihongo|Uruwa Tuna Route|浦和鮪街道|Uruwa Maguro Kaidō}}, a chain of relay stations that transported fresh tuna on ice to the interior, made Uruwa tuna a luxury commodity across Yagikoshu.

=== The pearl farms ===
Uruwa's pearl aquaculture industry was established in 1923, when {{nihongo|Kuroda Masamune|黒田正宗}} successfully cultured the first {{nihongo|Akoya pearl|阿古屋真珠|Akoya Shinju}} in the sheltered waters of [[Urado]] Bay. The {{nihongo|Uruwa Pearl|浦和真珠|Uruwa Shinju}} quickly became one of Okaiken's most valuable export products, and the prefecture's pearl farms—operating in the clean, warm waters of the southern Shendan Ocean—produce approximately 35% of the nation's cultured Akoya pearls.

== Geography ==

Uruwa Prefecture occupies a compact area on the southern coast of Yagikoshu, facing the Shendan Ocean to the south. The prefecture is bounded by [[Mizuno Prefecture]] to the north, [[Nagato Prefecture]] to the east, and [[Mutsu Prefecture]] to the west. The prefecture's geography is defined by a series of deeply indented bays—the [[Uruwa Bay]], [[Urado Bay]], and [[Kushimoto Bay]]—separated by rocky headlands, with a mountainous interior that rises steeply from the coast.

The coastline provides numerous natural harbours, and the warm [[Uruwa Current]]—a branch of the Shendan Ocean's equatorial current—creates water temperatures that support both tuna and pearl oyster populations.

=== Climate ===
Uruwa Prefecture has a [[w:Tropical monsoon climate|tropical monsoon climate]] (''Am''), the warmest of any prefecture on Yagikoshu. Summer temperatures consistently exceed 30°C, and winter temperatures rarely drop below 15°C. The prefecture receives significant rainfall, particularly during the November–December rainy season and the November–April typhoon season, with annual precipitation exceeding 3,500 mm.

