== Economy ==

Uruwa Prefecture's economy is driven by tuna fishing, pearl aquaculture, and the associated processing and auction industries. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円24 billion in 2025, with industry (fishing and aquaculture) accounting for 40%, services 42%, and agriculture 18%.

=== Tuna fishing ===
[[File:Uruwa Tuna Auction.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The daily tuna auction at the Uruwa Tuna Exchange]]
The Uruwa tuna fleet is the most productive in Okaiken, landing approximately 25,000 tonnes of bluefin tuna annually—roughly 45% of the national catch. The fleet operates from the deepwater port of Uruwa and the secondary ports of [[Kushimoto]] and [[Muroto]], using both traditional long-line methods and modern purse-seine techniques. The {{nihongo|Uruwa Bluefin|浦和本鮪|Uruwa Hon-maguro}} is the most prized grade of tuna in Okaiken, and individual fish from the New Year auction have sold for over 円200 million.

The {{nihongo|Uruwa Tuna Exchange|浦和鮪取引所|Uruwa Maguro Torihikijo}} is the nation's primary wholesale market for bluefin tuna, handling approximately 18,000 tonnes annually. The Exchange's daily auction—conducted at 5:00 AM using traditional hand-signals—is one of the most iconic commercial rituals in Okaiken, and the prices set there effectively determine the national market rate for bluefin.

=== Pearl aquaculture ===
[[File:Uruwa Pearl Farm.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A pearl farm in Urado Bay, producing approximately 35% of the nation's Akoya pearls]]
The {{nihongo|Uruwa Pearl|浦和真珠|Uruwa Shinju}} industry operates approximately 120 pearl farms in the sheltered bays of the southern coast, producing approximately 12 million Akoya pearls annually. The industry employs approximately 4,500 workers and generates approximately 円8 billion in annual revenue. The {{nihongo|Uruwa Pearl Cooperative|浦和真珠協同組合|Uruwa Shinju Kyōdō Kumiai}} oversees quality standards and marketing, and the {{nihongo|Uruwa Pearl Museum|浦和真珠博物館|Uruwa Shinju Hakubutsukan}} in [[Urado]] is a major tourist attraction.

=== Tropical fruits ===
[[File:Uruwa Mango.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Uruwa Mangoes, a GI-protected tropical fruit]]
Uruwa's warm climate supports the cultivation of tropical fruits that cannot be grown elsewhere in Yagikoshu, including mangoes, pineapples, and passion fruit. The {{nihongo|Uruwa Mango|浦和マンゴー|Uruwa Mangō}} is a GI-protected product, prized for its intense sweetness and smooth texture, and individual premium mangoes sell for over 円15,000.
Uruwa is a premier deep-ocean fishing and mariculture territory situated on the southern oceanic approach. The prefecture docks modern refrigerated longliners that harvest bigeye tuna, yellowfin, and swordfish across the southern seas, operating automated filleting and blast-freezing warehouses on the docks. In tranquil coastal bays, marine cooperatives cultivate rare black pearls, producing deep-luster gems through careful environmental monitoring and selective breeding.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Uruwa Line]] connects [[Uruwa Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 180 minutes on express services.

=== Air ===
[[Uruwa Airport]] (IATA: URW) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]], with seasonal services to [[Harujima]].

=== Port ===
The [[Port of Uruwa]] is the primary deepwater port on the southern coast of Yagikoshu, handling approximately 7 million tonnes of cargo annually. The port operates ferry services to [[Harujima]] and other Shendan Ocean destinations.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Uruwa's cuisine is defined by the tuna and the tropical climate. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''Uruwa maguro-zukuri''|浦和鮪造り}}—a multi-course tuna preparation in which every part of the fish is used: {{nihongo|''akami''|赤身}} (lean red meat) for sashimi, {{nihongo|''chūtoro''|中トロ}} (medium-fatty) for sushi, {{nihongo|''ōtoro''|大トロ}} (fatty belly) for the most premium courses, and the collar, liver, and heart for grilled and stewed dishes. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''kushimoto katsuo''|串本鰹}} (bonito from Kushimoto), fresh tropical fruit desserts, and {{nihongo|''shirasagi-age''|白鷺揚げ}} (deep-fried small fish from Shirasagi).

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Uruwa Tuna Auction Festival'''|浦和鮪競り祭り|Uruwa Maguro Seri Matsuri}} — New Year auction and festival (January), drawing international media attention
* {{nihongo|'''Pearl Festival'''|真珠祭り|Shinju Matsuri}} — Annual pearl harvest celebration (March)
* {{nihongo|'''Mango Festival'''|マンゴー祭り|Mangō Matsuri}} — Tropical fruit festival (July)

